      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "Given the equations of the lines, we can use the fact that the point of intersection $(6, 10)$ must satisfy both equations. Thus, substituting $x = 6$ and $y = 10$ into each equation should give us values for $m$ and $b$.\n\n1. For the first equation $y = mx + 4$:\n\\[\n\\begin{align*}\n10 &= 6m + 4\\\\\n6 &= 6m\\\\\nm  &= 1.\n\\end{align*}\n\\]\n\n2. For the second equation $y = 3x + b$:\n\\[\n\\begin{align*}\n10 &= 3(6) + b.\n\\end{align*}\n\\]\nCalculating this gives:\n\\[\n\\begin{align*}\n10 &= 18 + b\\\\\n-8 &= b.\n\\end{align*}\n\\]\n\nNow, to find $b + m$, we substitute our values of $b$ and $m$:\n\\[\n\\begin{align*}\nb + m &= -8 + 1\\\\\n&= -7.\n\\end{align*}\n\\]\n\nConclusion:\nThe value of $b + m$ is $\\boxed{-7}$.\n\nThis problem tests your ability to solve simultaneous equations, which is a fundamental skill when dealing with lines and intercepts in coordinate geometry.",
